    "seconds": 102,
    "summary": "I made one change that should clear the autoscaler alerts. I couldn't find a clear cause for the error and latency alerts, and I haven't confirmed that they cleared.\n\n**What I found**\n- **Traffic looks healthy:** frontend logs show only HTTP 200s, accounting is consuming orders normally, and every pod is Running. All the flagd fault-injection flags are `off`.\n- **The HPA alert:** the `KubeHpaMaxedOut` alert names the kube-state-metrics pod only because that pod reports the HPA metrics. The HPAs actually at max were `ad` and `fraud-detection` (10/10), plus `kafka` (10 of 10 by the time I checked).\n- **Why they maxed out:** these HPAs scale on memory utilization against low targets (frontend, fraud-detection and image-provider at 30%; ad, kafka and others at 80%). Several of these pods already use more memory than they request, for example `ad` at 116% of request and `fraud-detection` at 106%. Extra replicas don't lower per-pod memory, so the HPAs kept scaling out.\n- **Side effects:** the scale-out caused heavy pod churn, and some `kafka` pods restarted. That is probably what disturbed the frontend, frontend-proxy and accounting alerts, but I didn't confirm it.\n- **CPU scaling is broken for most services:** many of them have no CPU request, so the HPA events show \"missing request for cpu\". I didn't change that.\n\n**What I changed**\n- I raised the memory utilization target to 200% on the HPAs for `ad`, `fraud-detection`, `kafka`, `frontend` and `image-provider`.\n- All five now read below target, for example `ad` 116%/200% and `kafka` 89%/200%.\n\n**Still to do**\n- **Scale-down:** the replicas will shrink on their own after the default 5-minute stabilization window. I stopped before that happened, so `ad`, `fraud-detection` and `kafka` were still at 10 replicas when I last checked.\n- **Real fix:** set proper CPU and memory requests and sensible HPA targets in the Helm values. My patch is live-only, so a redeploy will revert it.",
